#
# Flings that stack smoothly, and a catch that is always a catch (on top of
# patch 0018).
#
# Measured on the Jolla Phone from the owner's own flicks (12 October 2026):
#
# - A finger that caught a coasting page and lifted with a few pixels of
#   motion (15 px/s) launched it at about 5500 px/s, to the end of the
#   page: any same-direction lift inherited the carry. Now only a flick of
#   at least PACIFIC_FLING_CARRY_MIN (default 0.5) of the caught speed
#   carries.
# - Stacking jumped: iOS's 0.000816 |v|^1.967 turned a 6000 px/s coast into
#   a 22000 px/s carry, so the second flick of a train hit the ceiling. A
#   flick now adds the caught speed (PACIFIC_FLING_CARRY_SHARE of it,
#   default 1): a train of flicks built 1863, 3212, 4165, 5069 px/s of carry
#   where it built 1568, 2695, 4644, 10752. The ceiling is 18000 px/s.
# - And only from a page still moving at 350 px/s or more
#   (PACIFIC_FLING_CARRY_FLOOR), as Chromium's fling boost.
# - The UI process modelled a fling from its own flick only, so a page
#   still coasting fast from a stacked fling was taken as stopped, and a
#   finger landing on it was not a catch. It now adds the same carry.
#
